The Big Picture: What We Built
We built a Customer Registry – a digital address book that stores customers and lets you manage them.

In Simple Terms:
Think of it like a phonebook app where you can:

Add new contacts

Find contacts by email

Delete contacts

See all contacts

The 4 Main Things We Did
#	What We Did	What It Does
1	Stored customers in a List	Holds all customer data in order
2	Used Generics (<Customer>)	Ensures only customers go in the list
3	Used Optional	Safely handles "customer not found"
4	Created Custom Exceptions	Gives specific error messages
What Each File Does
File	Purpose
Customer.java	A customer (has name, email, phone)
CustomerRegistry.java	The address book (add, find, delete)
DuplicateCustomerException.java	Error when adding a customer that already exists
CustomerNotFoundException.java	Error when trying to find/delete a customer that doesn't exist
RegistryMain.java	Runs the program to test it
CustomerRegistryTest.java	JUnit tests to verify everything works
Concept Breakdown
1. List (Collection)
java
private List<Customer> customers;
What: A container that holds multiple customers

Why: We need to store many customers, not just one

How: Each customer gets added to the list

2. Generics (<Customer>)
java
private List<Customer> customers;
What: Tells Java this list can ONLY hold Customer objects

Why: Prevents mistakes (can't add a String or Integer by accident)

How: <Customer> is the generic type

3. Optional
java
public Optional<Customer> findByEmail(String email)
What: A box that may or may not contain a customer

Why: Instead of returning null (which can crash), we return Optional.empty() when not found

How: Caller must check if the customer exists using .isPresent() or .ifPresent()

4. Custom Exceptions
java
public class DuplicateCustomerException extends RuntimeException
What: Our own error type

Why: Gives clear, specific error messages

How: We throw it when something goes wrong (like duplicate email)

How It All Works Together
text
1. User adds a customer → stored in List
2. User finds by email → Optional returns customer or empty
3. User tries to add duplicate → throws DuplicateCustomerException
4. User tries to delete missing customer → throws CustomerNotFoundException
5. Program prints all customers from List
What We Tested
Test	What It Checks
Add customer	Customer is added to registry
Add duplicate	Throws DuplicateCustomerException
Find by email	Returns the correct customer
Find not found	Returns Optional.empty()
Delete by email	Removes customer from registry
Delete not found	Throws CustomerNotFoundException
Why This Matters for Backend Development
Concept	Real Backend Use
List	Storing records from database
Generics	Type-safe data handling
Optional	Safely handling missing data from database
Custom Exceptions	Proper error handling in API responses